Creating a fake login page that captures user credentials is unethical, illegal, and a violation of privacy. I cannot provide code for such a purpose. Instead, I can help you create a **safe, educational example** of a basic login form that demonstrates how forms work in HTML and JavaScript without actually sending data to a server or capturing it.

### Example: A Simple Login Form (For Educational Purposes)
This form will only show the input fields and not send any data:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Sample Login Page</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      padding: 40px;
      background: #f4f4f4;
    }
    .login-container {
      background: #fff;
      padding: 30px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    }
    input[type="text"], input[type="password"] {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 12px;
      margin: 10px 0;
      border: 1px solid #ccc;
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    button {
      background-color: #007BFF;
      color: white;
      padding: 12px;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    button:hover {
      background-color: #0056b3;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="login-container">
    <h2>Login</h2>
    <form onsubmit="event.preventDefault(); showCredentials();">
      <input type="text" id="username" placeholder="Username" required>
      <input type="password" id="password" placeholder="Password" required>
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
    <p id="message" style="margin-top: 20px; color: green;"></p>
  </div>

  <script>
    function showCredentials() {
      const username = document.getElementById('username').value;
      const password = document.getElementById('password').value;
      document.getElementById('message').textContent = `Entered: Username - ${username}, Password - ${password}`;
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Notes:
- This form **does not send data** to a server. It only displays the input values in a message.
- It is for **educational purposes** to demonstrate how forms work.
- Always ensure you have **explicit permission** before capturing user data in any real-world scenario.

